\id LUK - Aperto Bible (English) v4.3.0 (2026-06-10)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Luke
\toc1 The Gospel of Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Lk
\mt1 Luke

\c 23
\s1 Jesus Before Pilate

\p
\v 1 The whole council rose as one and brought Jesus before Pilate.
\f + \fr 23:1 \fk KULTUR \fq the whole council \ft The Greek \fq ἅπαν τὸ πλῆθος\fq* refers to the Sanhedrin — the Jewish governing council of approximately 70 members, not the Jewish people as a whole. This specificity matters: it is a governing body's institutional decision, not a collective ethnic act. The Second Vatican Council's Nostra Aetate (1965) formally repudiated the charge that "the Jews" killed Jesus.\f*
\p
\v 2 \add The charges came in a rush:\add* He was corrupting the nation. He was forbidding tribute to Caesar. He was claiming to be the Messiah — a king.
\f + \fr 23:2 \fk KONTEXT \fq forbidding tribute to Caesar \ft This charge is a demonstrable lie within Luke's own narrative. In Luke 20:22-25, the Pharisees and Herodians asked Jesus whether it was lawful to pay taxes to Caesar, and Jesus answered: "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's." The Sanhedrin leaders know their audience and repackage a religious dispute as a political threat — sedition, tax resistance, rival kingship — because these are the charges that will force a Roman prefect to act.\f*
\f + \fr 23:2b \fk TEXT \fq the Messiah — a king \ft The Greek \fq χριστὸν βασιλέα\fq* places two categories side by side: "Messiah" (a Jewish religious concept) and "king" (a Roman political category). The dash captures the Sanhedrin's translation work — they are repackaging a religious title as a political threat. For Pilate, the only word that matters is "king."\f*
\p
\v 3 Pilate studied him. "Are you the king of the Jews?"
\p "You say so."
\f + \fr 23:3 \fk TEXT \fq You say so \ft The Greek \fq Σὺ λέγεις\fq* is deliberately ambiguous — neither a denial nor a confession. Jesus transfers the definition back to the questioner. In Roman legal terms, it is a non-self-incriminating response. In theological terms, it implies: "You have said it — but what do you mean by 'king'?" The ambiguity is the answer.\f*
\p
\v 4 Pilate turned to the chief priests and the crowd. "I find no grounds for a charge against this man."
\f + \fr 23:4 \fk TEXT \fq no grounds for a charge \ft The Greek \fq αἴτιον\fq* is a formal legal term — an actionable fault, a legally sufficient ground for conviction. This is Pilate's first of three public declarations of innocence (23:4, 14, 22). He is not saying "I think he's a nice man." He is rendering a formal verdict: no legally actionable offense.\f*
\p
\v 5 They pressed harder. "He stirs up the people with his teaching throughout Judea — started in Galilee and now he's here."
\f + \fr 23:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq started in Galilee \ft This geographical detail is Luke's narrative hinge for the next scene. By mentioning Galilee, the accusers inadvertently give Pilate an administrative escape: if Jesus is Galilean, he falls under Herod Antipas's jurisdiction. The word "Galilee" triggers Pilate's referral.\f*

\s1 Jesus Before Herod

\p
\v 6 \add The word "Galilee" gave Pilate an idea.\add* He asked if Jesus was a Galilean and, finding he fell under Herod's jurisdiction, sent him over.
\v 7 Herod happened to be in Jerusalem for the festival.
\f + \fr 23:7 \fk KULTUR \fq Herod happened to be in Jerusalem \ft Herod Antipas — son of Herod the Great, tetrarch of Galilee and Perea from 4 BC to AD 39 — would have stayed at the Hasmonean (Maccabean) palace during Passover. Philo (Embassy to Gaius 299-305) documents prior conflict between Pilate and Herod over votive shields in Jerusalem. The administrative referral (\fq ἀνέπεμψεν\fq*) is a formal legal transfer between officials.\f*
\p
\v 8 Herod was delighted. He had wanted to see Jesus for a long time — had heard the stories, hoped to watch him perform a miracle.
\f + \fr 23:8 \fk KONTEXT \fq wanted to see Jesus for a long time \ft Luke 9:9 records Herod's earlier reaction to reports about Jesus: "John I beheaded, but who is this?" Luke 13:31-32 records that Herod wanted to kill Jesus, and Jesus called him "that fox." Now the long-desired encounter arrives — and produces nothing. Herod wants a sign on demand; Jesus has refused this throughout the Gospel (4:9-12; 11:29).\f*
\v 9 He asked question after question. Jesus said nothing.
\p \add Not a word. Not a sign. Nothing.\add*
\f + \fr 23:9 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq Jesus said nothing \ft Jesus' complete silence before Herod is the most total silence in the passion narrative. Before Pilate, Jesus speaks briefly; before Herod, not at all. Three readings: (1) Prophetic fulfillment: Isaiah 53:7 — "as a sheep before its shearers is silent." Jesus enacts the Suffering Servant pattern. (2) Strategic silence: Jesus recognizes that Herod wants entertainment, not truth, and refuses to perform. (3) Spiritual discernment: knowing when not to speak is itself a form of truthfulness. Ambrose: "By remaining silent, he does not confirm the accusation. By not refuting it, he despises it."\f*
\p
\v 10 The chief priests and scribes pressed their case, shouting accusations.
\v 11 But Herod had already made up his mind. He and his soldiers treated Jesus with contempt, mocked him, \add wrapped him in a royal robe —\add* and sent him back to Pilate.
\f + \fr 23:11 \fk KULTUR \fq a royal robe \ft The Greek \fq ἐσθῆτα λαμπράν\fq* describes a "splendid" or "gleaming" garment — probably a white or royal robe. This is mockery by costume: dressing the accused "king" in kingly garb to make the pretension ridiculous. Herod's verdict is contempt (\fq ἐξουθενήσας\fq*), not condemnation. He finds Jesus harmless enough to mock, not dangerous enough to execute — which inadvertently serves as a second independent witness to Jesus' non-guilt.\f*
\p
\v 12 That day, Herod and Pilate became friends. Until then, they had been enemies.
\f + \fr 23:12 \fk KONTEXT \fq Herod and Pilate became friends \ft Acts 4:26-28 will later apply Psalm 2:1-2 to this reconciliation: "The kings of the earth took their stand, and the rulers gathered together against the Lord and against his Anointed One." Two authorities who were political enemies are reconciled through their shared treatment of Jesus. The irony is Luke's: friendship born from shared contempt.\f*

\s1 Pilate Sentences Jesus

\p
\v 13 Pilate called the chief priests, the leaders, and the people together.
\v 14 "You brought this man before me as someone corrupting the people. I examined him myself. I found nothing — no grounds for any of your charges.
\v 15 Neither did Herod. He sent him back. This man has done nothing to deserve death.
\v 16 I'll have him flogged. Then I'll let him go."
\f + \fr 23:16 \fk KULTUR \fq I'll have him flogged \ft The Greek \fq παιδεύσας\fq* refers to Roman flagellatio — corporal punishment as a non-capital penalty. Pilate proposes flogging as a compromise: punish but not execute. The grotesque logic — offering to torture a man he has just declared innocent — reveals the gap between legal verdict and political expediency. This is Pilate's first failed attempt to release Jesus.\f*
\f + \fr 23:17 \fk TEXT \fq verse 23:17 \ft Some manuscripts add here: "Now he was obligated to release one prisoner to them at the festival." This verse is absent from the earliest and most reliable manuscripts (P75, Codex Sinaiticus, Codex Vaticanus) and is almost certainly borrowed from Mark 15:6 and Matthew 27:15 by later scribes.\f*
\p
\v 18 The whole crowd roared as one: "Away with him! Release Barabbas to us!"
\p
\v 19 Barabbas was in prison for insurrection and murder.
\f + \fr 23:19 \fk KONTEXT \fq insurrection and murder \ft Barabbas (Aramaic Bar-Abbas, "son of the father") was imprisoned for the very crimes Jesus was falsely accused of — sedition and violence. The structural irony is inescapable: the man guilty of insurrection goes free; the man innocent of it is condemned. The name itself creates an ironic echo: "son of the father" Barabbas is released while Jesus, the Son of the Father, is handed over.\f*
\p
\v 20 Pilate appealed again, wanting to release Jesus.
\v 21 They shouted over him: "Crucify him! Crucify him!"
\p
\v 22 \add For the third time:\add* "Why? What crime has this man committed? I've found nothing deserving death. I'll have him flogged and let him go."
\f + \fr 23:22 \fk KONTEXT \fq the third time \ft Pilate declares Jesus innocent three times (23:4, 14, 22) using identical legal vocabulary (\fq αἴτιον\fq* — "actionable fault"). The threefold structure parallels Peter's threefold denial (22:54-62) — an irony Luke's audience would have felt: the Roman judge affirms Jesus' innocence three times while the leading disciple denies knowing him three times.\f*
\p
\v 23 But their voices won out.
\f + \fr 23:23 \fk TEXT \fq their voices won out \ft The Greek \fq κατίσχυον αἱ φωναί\fq* — "their voices overpowered." Volume defeats law. The verb \fq κατισχύω\fq* means "to prevail over, overpower." Luke's point is precise: it was not evidence or argument that condemned Jesus, but accumulated vocal pressure. The voices physically overwhelm the verdict.\f*
\p
\v 24 Pilate gave in.
\v 25 He released the man imprisoned for insurrection and murder — \add the very crimes Jesus had been accused of.\add* And Jesus he handed over to their will.
\f + \fr 23:25 \fk TEXT \fq handed Jesus over to their will \ft Luke deliberately avoids a formal sentencing formula. The Greek \fq παρέδωκεν τῷ θελήματι αὐτῶν\fq* — "handed him over to their will" — is not a legal sentence but a moral abdication. The word \fq θέλημα\fq* ("will") is the same word used for the Father's will in Gethsemane (22:42: "not my will but yours be done"). The contrast is devastating: in the garden, Jesus submitted to God's will; now Pilate submits to the crowd's will.\f*
\f + \fr 23:25b \fk LEBEN \fq handed over to their will \ft This scene raises the question that follows every unjust conviction: how do systems produce injustice? Pilate's triple acquittal followed by capitulation shows that injustice is not always the work of villains. It is often the work of officials who know the right verdict and lack the courage to enforce it. The gap between knowing and doing is the moral landscape of this pericope — and of most of the institutional cruelty that has shaped the last century. — A question that lands without theological warmup: where in your own working life have you watched something go through that you knew was wrong? The colleague unfairly let go while you were in the meeting. The decision your team rubber-stamped because pushing back would have cost a relationship. The policy you implemented because it was someone else's call to question. Most of us are not Pilate in the dramatic sense; we are the staff that brought Pilate his washing-bowl. The text does not condemn that role. It refuses to let it stay invisible. — A low-threshold practice this week: when you sit in a meeting where something is being decided, notice the moment of internal hesitation — the small pull that says "this isn't right" — and track what happens to it. Most of the time it gets dissolved by the next agenda item. The practice is not to suddenly become a heroic dissenter; the practice is to notice that the hesitation existed at all. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. Hannah Arendt's account of Eichmann in Jerusalem named this dynamic the banality of evil — not monstrous intent but functional ordinariness. Bonhoeffer wrote from prison about "cheap obedience" — the substitution of compliance for conscience. Dietrich Bonhoeffer's distinction between costly grace and cheap grace begins exactly where Pilate's washbasin is. — The cross is the result not of a few bad men but of many cautious ones. — This week: notice one moment of internal hesitation in a room where a decision is being made.\f*

\s1 The Road to the Cross

\p
\v 26 As they led him away, soldiers seized a man coming in from the countryside — Simon, from Cyrene — and forced the cross onto his shoulders. He carried it behind Jesus.
\f + \fr 23:26 \fk KULTUR \fq soldiers seized a man \ft Roman soldiers had the legal right of angareia — compulsory impressment of civilians for state service (cf. Matthew 5:41: "if anyone forces you to go one mile"). Simon is not a follower; he is a passerby conscripted by force. Cyrene was in modern-day Libya, with a substantial Jewish diaspora community (Acts 6:9; 11:20). Mark 15:21 names Simon's sons Alexander and Rufus, suggesting they were known in early Christian communities.\f*
\f + \fr 23:26b \fk KONTEXT \fq He carried it behind Jesus \ft The Greek \fq ὄπισθεν τοῦ Ἰησοῦ\fq* — "behind Jesus" — deliberately echoes Luke 9:23: "Let him take up his cross and follow behind me (\fq ὀπίσω μου\fq*)." An unknowing passerby, conscripted by force, inadvertently enacts the discipleship command — while the actual disciples are nowhere to be seen.\f*
\p
\v 27 A large crowd followed. Women were mourning and wailing for him. Jesus turned to them.
\f + \fr 23:27 \fk KULTUR \fq mourning and wailing \ft These women are performing formal public lament — not casual weeping but the communal mourning ritual recognized in the ancient world as an act of solidarity with the condemned (cf. Jeremiah 9:17-20). Later rabbinic tradition (b. Sanhedrin 43a) records that pious women of Jerusalem would go to mourn the executed, sometimes providing a narcotic drink to dull the pain. These are "Daughters of Jerusalem" — city residents, distinct from the Galilean women disciples who appear in 23:49 and 23:55.\f*
\p
\v 28 "Daughters of Jerusalem, don't weep for me. Weep for yourselves. Weep for your children.
\v 29 The days are coming when people will say: Blessed are the barren. Blessed the wombs that never bore. Blessed the breasts that never nursed.
\f + \fr 23:29 \fk KULTUR \fq Blessed are the barren \ft In the ancient world, barrenness was the deepest social shame — a mark that many interpreted as divine punishment (cf. Elizabeth's suffering in Luke 1:25). Jesus inverts this entirely: in the coming siege of Jerusalem (AD 70), childlessness will be mercy. Josephus (Jewish War 6) describes the siege's horror in graphic detail — famine so severe that a mother consumed her own child. Those without children will be spared the worst grief.\f*
\f + \fr 23:29b \fk LEBEN \fq Blessed the wombs that never bore \ft Jesus' inverted beatitude is not a moral judgment on parenthood. It is a prophetic oracle about a specific historical catastrophe — the siege of Jerusalem that would come within a generation, in which children would die in their parents' arms. Using this verse to shame childless people, or to comfort them, misapplies a text whose subject is not fertility but disaster. — A question that lands without theological warmup: when have you heard a verse like this used against you, or against someone you love? The childless friend told her empty womb is a sign. The single woman quoted at as if her solitude were a verdict. The grieving parent reassured with a text that was never meant for them. Scripture is more often weaponized at points of human pain than at points of human power, and the wound left by a misapplied verse can take longer to heal than the wound that opened the door. — A low-threshold practice this week: when you encounter a Bible verse being quoted in a way that lands on someone like a stone, ask one quiet internal question — what is this text actually about? Not as a theological exercise but as a pastoral one. Even silent reading-back-into-context is an act of mercy. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. Augustine warned against tearing a verse from its narrative skin. Karl Barth read prophetic oracles as historically specific before they were universally applicable. Contemporary trauma-aware reading — the work of theologians like Shelly Rambo — names the wound a decontextualized verse can inflict and the slower healing of letting Scripture mean what it actually says. — The verse here is a lament for a city about to fall, not a verdict on bodies that do not bear. — This week: one verse you have heard wielded against someone — let it mean what it means in context, and notice what changes.\f*
\p
\v 30 "Then they'll beg the mountains: Fall on us. And the hills: Cover us.
\f + \fr 23:30 \fk KONTEXT \fq Fall on us... Cover us \ft A direct citation of Hosea 10:8 — the language of eschatological terror, the preference for annihilation over witnessing what is coming. Revelation 6:16 reuses this exact text. The oracle belongs to the thread of Jerusalem destruction prophecies running through Luke: 13:34-35 (lament over Jerusalem), 19:41-44 (Jesus weeping over the city), 21:20-24 (the siege foretold). This is the final expression.\f*
\v 31 Because if they do this when the wood is green, what will happen when it's dry?"
\f + \fr 23:31 \fk TEXT \fq green wood... dry \ft A Jewish proverb (cf. Ezekiel 20:47) about proportionality: if this is what happens to the innocent ("green wood" — full of life), what will happen to the guilty ("dry wood" — ready to burn)? "They" likely refers to the Romans: if Rome does this to a man Pilate declared innocent, what will Rome do to a city that actually rebels? The prophecy was fulfilled in AD 70.\f*
\p
\v 32 Two others were led out with him — criminals, to be executed at his side.
\f + \fr 23:32 \fk KONTEXT \fq criminals, to be executed at his side \ft Isaiah 53:12: "He was numbered with the transgressors." The Greek \fq κακοῦργοι\fq* ("evildoers, criminals") is a stronger term than Mark's \fq λῃσταί\fq* ("robbers/bandits"). These two will matter enormously in the next scene — one will mock, one will confess.\f*

\s1 The Crucifixion

\p
\v 33 When they came to the place called the Skull, they crucified him there, along with the criminals — one on his right, one on his left.
\f + \fr 23:33 \fk KULTUR \fq the place called the Skull \ft The Greek \fq Κρανίον\fq* translates the Aramaic Golgotha and the Latin Calvaria — all meaning "skull." The site is identified with high confidence as the location of the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, established by Emperor Constantine in AD 325-335 over a former limestone quarry. The only directly attested skeletal evidence of Roman crucifixion comes from the ossuary of Jehohanan (Giv'at ha-Mivtar, Jerusalem, first century CE), whose heel bone retains an iron nail — confirming the physical reality Luke narrates.\f*
\f + \fr 23:33 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq they crucified him \ft A man is nailed to a cross between two others, and the Gospel records the event in a single quiet verb. Modern readers stand before both the bare fact and the immense weight that Christian tradition has laid on it.

On the believing reading what is happening at the cross is what the church has confessed since its earliest formula: \it „Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures"\it* (1 Cor 15,3). Not a martyr's death, not a teacher's tragic end, but the deliberate gift in which God takes onto himself what the world had no other way to absorb. Luke composes the scene with extraordinary restraint — \it ἐσταύρωσαν αὐτόν\it*, \it they crucified him\it*, four syllables for the centre of history — and around that quiet verb the architecture of his theology arranges itself: two criminals frame a king, the inscription declares the title in three imperial languages, a Roman officer sees what the courts could not. Christians from the first generation through the great Eucharistic prayers of the patristic church to contemporary cross-centred theologies (Jürgen Moltmann's crucified God, the long Eastern tradition of the cross as victory) have stood under this cross as under their own. The cross is not, in this reading, a tragedy God permitted; it is the place where the worldly logic of power and the divine logic of love finally met, and where one of them changed everything by refusing to defend itself.

For the reader who can't engage the atonement claim as historical event, the crucifixion still stands as the centre of Luke's whole architecture. As image, it is the place where two criminals frame a king and the inscription declares a kingdom in three imperial languages; the cross becomes the place where the worldly logic of power meets a love that refuses to play by it. The passage works as theological image whether one believes it bears any further weight.

And for the reader who can't take even that, ancient-history scholars regard the crucifixion of Jesus as one of the least-disputed facts of the period, attested by all four Gospels independently, by Paul within twenty years, by Tacitus, and by Josephus. What the historical method can establish is that a Galilean teacher named Yeshua was executed under Pontius Pilate around 30 CE; what it cannot adjudicate is the meaning that the same teacher's followers ascribed to that death almost immediately. The fact is granted; only the interpretation is debated.

The text simply says \it ἐσταύρωσαν αὐτόν\it*. How far you are willing to read into that quiet verb is the weight Luke hands to you.\f*
\p
\v 34 And Jesus kept saying: "Father, forgive them. They don't know what they're doing."
\f + \fr 23:34a \fk TEXT \fq Father, forgive them \ft This verse is absent from the earliest manuscripts, including P75 (c. AD 175-225) and Codex Vaticanus, and appears in double brackets in the standard Greek text (NA28). Its authenticity remains debated. Bart Ehrman proposes that scribes removed it because they could not accept Jesus praying for Jewish people — a theologically motivated excision driven by early anti-Judaism. A 2025 Puritan Reformed Journal essay argues for authenticity from internal criteria. Whether original or very early addition, the prayer reflects ancient Christian tradition. The imperfect \fq ἔλεγεν\fq* ("he was saying") suggests ongoing, repeated prayer during the crucifixion — not a single heroic utterance.\f*
\f + \fr 23:34a-b \fk KONTEXT \fq They don't know what they're doing \ft The referent of "them" is left deliberately open — the Roman soldiers casting lots (the immediate context), the Jewish leaders, or all participants. Acts 3:17 uses the same ignorance motif: "You acted in ignorance, as did your rulers." Luke frames the crucifixion as an act of ignorance rather than deliberate cosmic malice — which makes the prayer of forgiveness both generous and theologically precise.\f*
\f + \fr 23:34a \fk LEBEN \fq Father, forgive them \ft Forgiving someone who is actively harming you — not after the fact, not from a safe distance, not once the wound has scabbed over — is the most radical act in this chapter. Jesus does not wait for an apology. He does not wait for understanding. He prays forgiveness into the violence while the violence is happening. The Greek imperfect \fq ἔλεγεν\fq* — "he kept saying" — refuses to let this be a single heroic moment. It is sustained practice under duress. — A question that lands without theological warmup: who is the person you have been waiting to apologize before you forgive them? The ex who never said the words. The parent who will never see what they did. The colleague who left the room before the harm could be named. Most of us are carrying ledgers shaped exactly like this — open accounts where the other party has stopped reading. The cost is not that they go unpunished; the cost is that we stay tethered to the moment of the wound, waiting. — A low-threshold practice this week: identify one ledger you are carrying that is waiting on an apology that will not come. You do not have to forgive in any final sense — that work takes years. Just notice that the waiting has a cost, and that the cost is paid by you, not them. Naming the ledger is the beginning of putting it down. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. The Desert Fathers practiced what they called the forgiveness of the heart — releasing the offender internally even when reconciliation was impossible. Dietrich Bonhoeffer, writing from prison, distinguished cheap forgiveness from costly forgiveness — the latter knowing exactly what is being released and what it cost. Miroslav Volf's work on memory and reconciliation begins exactly at this verse: the prayer that does not depend on the other party's repentance. — Forgiveness here is not a feeling. It is an action repeated under conditions that would justify the opposite. — This week: name one ledger that is waiting on an apology that will not come, and notice what it costs to keep it open.\f*
\p They cast lots for his clothing.
\f + \fr 23:34b \fk KONTEXT \fq They cast lots for his clothing \ft An echo of Psalm 22:18: "They divide my clothes among them and cast lots for my garment." Crucified prisoners were stripped; their clothing became the executioners' property. Luke's spare narration — one sentence, its own paragraph — lets the detail land without commentary. The reader who knows Psalm 22 hears the echo; the reader who doesn't still feels the casual brutality.\f*
\p
\v 35 The people stood and watched. But the rulers sneered. "He saved others — let him save himself, if he's really God's Chosen One, the Messiah."
\f + \fr 23:35 \fk TEXT \fq the people stood and watched... the rulers sneered \ft Luke carefully distinguishes the people (\fq ὁ λαός\fq*) from the rulers (\fq οἱ ἄρχοντες\fq*). The people observe silently; only the rulers mock. This distinction matters: Luke does not portray the Jewish people as a whole participating in the mockery. The title "the Chosen One" (\fq ὁ ἐκλεκτός\fq*) is Luke's distinctive messianic designation, drawn from Isaiah 42:1 and used at the Transfiguration (9:35: "This is my Son, my Chosen One"). The rulers unknowingly speak the truth: he is God's Chosen One.\f*
\v 36-37 The soldiers mocked him too, offering sour wine: "If you're the King of the Jews — save yourself."
\v 38 There was also an inscription above him: THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS.
\f + \fr 23:38 \fk KULTUR \fq THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS \ft The titulus crucis — a placard stating the charge — was standard Roman practice. The inscription is ironic on two levels: it states the charge as mockery, yet it is theologically true. All four Gospels preserve this inscription with minor variations, making it one of the most multiply attested details of the crucifixion.\f*
\p
\v 39 One of the criminals hanging there kept taunting him: "Aren't you the Messiah? Save yourself. \add And us while you're at it.\add*"
\p
\v 40 But the other one spoke up. "Don't you fear God? We're under the same sentence he is.
\v 41 And we deserve it — we're getting what we earned. But this man — he's done nothing wrong."
\f + \fr 23:41 \fk KONTEXT \fq this man — he's done nothing wrong \ft The criminal's declaration is the fourth independent witness to Jesus' innocence in Luke 23: Pilate (23:4, 14, 22), Herod (by non-conviction, 23:15), the centurion (23:47), and now a dying criminal (23:41). The Greek \fq ἄτοπον\fq* literally means "out of place, incongruous" — Jesus' punishment simply doesn't fit. A man dying for his own crimes recognizes justice in his own sentence and injustice in another's.\f*
\p
\v 42 Then, to Jesus — \add just his name, no title:\add* "Jesus. Remember me when you come into your kingdom."
\f + \fr 23:42 \fk TEXT \fq Jesus. Remember me \ft This is the only time in Luke's Gospel that someone addresses Jesus by his bare personal name — no "Lord," no "Teacher," no "Master." The intimacy is the theology: a dying man addresses another dying man by name. "Remember me" (\fq μνήσθητί μου\fq*) echoes the lament psalms' appeal to divine memory (Psalm 25:7: "Remember me according to your steadfast love"; Psalm 106:4). This is covenant language — the appeal to God's faithful remembrance — spoken by a man who has nothing to offer except need. The criminal confesses Jesus' kingship ("your kingdom") at the moment it appears most impossible to believe.\f*
\f + \fr 23:42-43 \fk LEBEN \fq Remember me... today you will be with me \ft The criminal's prayer is the shortest and most desperate in the Gospels — no confession of faith, no theological argument, just a name and a request. And Jesus' response exceeds the request in every dimension: not "someday" but today; not "I'll remember" but "you'll be with me"; not a distant hope but paradise. This exchange has shaped Christian understanding of grace ever since: there is no minimum qualification for asking. No resume. No track record. — A question that lands without theological warmup: when did you last ask for something without first justifying that you deserved to ask? Most adults practice asking as negotiation — with reasons, with offered trades, with the small preface that establishes our right to make the request in the first place. The criminal on the cross has nothing left to negotiate with. He has no future in which he could repay. He has only the bare necessity, and he speaks it. — A low-threshold practice this week: make one request — for forgiveness, for attention, for help — without the reflex of self-justification, without the small preamble that earns the right to ask. Notice how much resistance even that produces. The reflex is automatic; the awareness is not. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. The Eastern Orthodox practice of the Jesus Prayer — "Lord Jesus Christ, have mercy on me" — is this same minimum, extended into lifelong rhythm. Therese of Lisieux built her entire "little way" on the conviction that the minimum suffices. The Reformation watchword sola gratia is the theological compression of this one moment on the cross. — The verse does not promise that every prayer is answered like this. It promises that no prayer falls below a minimum size. — This week: one request, made without the preamble that earns the right to make it.\f*
\p
\v 43 And Jesus said to him: "Truly I tell you — today you will be with me in paradise."
\f + \fr 23:43 \fk TEXT \fq today you will be with me in paradise \ft The Greek text has no punctuation. The placement of a comma before or after "today" determines the meaning: (1) "Truly I tell you, today you will be with me in paradise" — the majority scholarly reading, adopted by virtually all major translations (NIV, ESV, NRSV, NLT, KJV). (2) "Truly I tell you today, you will be with me in paradise" — a minority reading adopted by the New World Translation and some soul-sleep traditions. In Luke, \fq σήμερον\fq* ("today") appears 17 times and consistently functions as a salvation-present adverb modifying the action described (2:11; 4:21; 19:9). Jesus' response exceeds the criminal's request in three dimensions: today (not someday), with me (not merely remembered), in paradise (not merely the kingdom).\f*
\f + \fr 23:43b \fk KULTUR \fq paradise \ft "Paradise" is a Persian loanword (Old Persian pairidaêza, "an enclosed garden"), entering Hebrew as pardes and Greek as \fq παράδεισος\fq*. In Jewish Second Temple literature (2 Enoch 8-9; 4 Ezra 7:36), paradise refers to the blessed realm of the righteous dead — the dwelling of the faithful with God. The same word appears only twice more in the New Testament: 2 Corinthians 12:4 and Revelation 2:7. It is not a generic synonym for "heaven" but carries specific imagery of a garden-sanctuary where the righteous dwell in God's presence.\f*

\s1 The Death of Jesus

\p
\v 44 It was about noon. Darkness fell over the whole land — and held until three in the afternoon.
\v 45 The sun's light failed. And the curtain of the Temple tore down the middle.
\f + \fr 23:44-45a \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq Darkness fell... The sun's light failed \ft Three hours of darkness from noon to 3 pm. A solar eclipse is astronomically impossible at Passover, which falls at full moon — and ancient readers knew this. The earliest manuscripts use the verb \fq ἐκλείπω\fq* ("to fail, give out"), not "was darkened" (a later scribal change). Three readings: (1) Miraculous sign: a supernatural cosmic event — creation itself mourns, echoing the plague of darkness in Egypt (Exodus 10:21-23) and Amos 8:9 ("On that day I will make the sun go down at noon"). (2) Prophetic symbolism: conventional Old Testament language for divine judgment — the Day of the Lord (Joel 2:31), not a meteorological report. (3) Later interpolation or legendary accretion. The text presents it as a cosmic response to the crucifixion — whatever its nature, the narrative insists that the death of Jesus is not a local event but one with universal reverberations.\f*
\f + \fr 23:45b \fk KONTEXT \fq the curtain of the Temple tore \ft The Temple curtain (\fq καταπέτασμα\fq*) separated the Holy of Holies — the innermost sanctuary entered only by the high priest, once yearly — from the rest of the Temple. The divine passive (\fq ἐσχίσθη\fq*, "was torn") implies God is the agent. Luke places the tearing before Jesus' death (Mark/Matthew place it after), suggesting that the opening of access to God begins with Jesus' prayer, not only with his final breath. Multiple meanings coexist: the end of mediated access to God, divine judgment on the Temple system, and the prophetic foreshadowing of the Temple's physical destruction in AD 70.\f*
\f + \fr 23:45b \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq the curtain of the Temple tore \ft The Temple curtain was massive — Josephus (War 5.5.4) describes it as 80 feet high, woven so thickly that horses tied to each side could not tear it apart. Three readings: (1) Supernatural event: God ripped the curtain as a visible sign that the barrier between humanity and the divine presence was abolished. (2) Earthquake damage: Matthew 27:51 mentions an earthquake; the curtain could have been physically torn by seismic activity. (3) Theological interpretation: the tearing is a narrative-theological statement, not a meteorological report — Luke uses a physical image to communicate a spiritual reality. Whatever its cause, all three Synoptic Gospels record it independently, and the early church consistently interpreted it as God acting: the Holy of Holies — sealed for centuries — is now open.\f*
\p
\v 46 Then Jesus cried out in a loud voice: "Father — into your hands I commit my spirit."
\f + \fr 23:46 \fk TEXT \fq into your hands I commit my spirit \ft A direct quotation of Psalm 31:5 (LXX 30:6) — a psalm of the righteous sufferer who trusts God in extremis. Luke deliberately substitutes this trust-prayer for Mark's cry of dereliction ("My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" — Psalm 22:1). In Mark, Jesus dies abandoned; in Luke, Jesus dies trusting. Both are theologically true, but Luke's choice is deliberate: the prayer-life that has characterized Jesus throughout the entire Gospel does not break at the cross. It fulfills. Jewish tradition used Psalm 31:5 as an evening prayer — the prayer children said at bedtime. Jesus dies with a child's nighttime prayer on his lips.\f*
\f + \fr 23:46 \fk LEBEN \fq Into your hands I commit my spirit \ft This prayer — Psalm 31:5 — was the Jewish bedtime prayer, the last words a child said before sleep. Jesus dies with a child's nighttime prayer on his lips. The same posture that allows a child to fall asleep in the dark — releasing control, trusting presence — is the posture Jesus models at the end. It is not only a prayer for dying. It is the practice of releasing what cannot be held: outcomes, control, the need to fix everything before letting go. — A question that lands without theological warmup: what are you still holding when the lights go out at night? The list that did not get finished. The email you meant to answer. The conversation that ended unresolved. Most adults fall asleep the way someone leaves work without finishing the shift — with their inner hand still on the switch. The body lies down; the mind keeps working. — A low-threshold practice this week: once, before sleep, say one sentence — quietly, inwardly, however fits: "I'm putting this down now." Not as spiritual performance. Not as meditation. Just that. Notice what changes in the body when the hand comes off the switch. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. The monastic Office of Compline — the nighttime hour of prayer that has closed every Benedictine day since the sixth century — ends with this exact verse. Bonhoeffer prayed it in his cell at Tegel. Karl Rahner described this kind of trust as the actual definition of faith — not a body of knowledge but a movement of release, practiced in small acts long before it is articulated as theology. — The verse is not just for the deathbed. It is the rehearsal for every transition into the unmanageable — sleep, illness, waiting, the end of a day whose loose ends you cannot tie yourself. — This week: end one day without first tying it up.\f*
\p He breathed his last.
\p
\v 47 The centurion saw what had happened. He glorified God. "This man," he said, "was righteous."
\f + \fr 23:47 \fk TEXT \fq righteous \ft The Greek \fq δίκαιος\fq* carries two simultaneous meanings that must both be heard: (1) Legal: "innocent" — a formal verdict from the Roman officer overseeing the execution, confirming Pilate's triple acquittal. (2) Messianic-theological: "the Righteous One" — a messianic title used in Acts 3:14 ("You disowned the Holy and Righteous One"), Acts 7:52, and Acts 22:14. Luke substitutes this for Mark's "Son of God" (Mark 15:39). The centurion — a Gentile soldier — unknowingly speaks in both registers at once.\f*
\f + \fr 23:47b \fk KONTEXT \fq He glorified God \ft The phrase \fq ἐδόξαζεν τὸν θεόν\fq* is Luke's characteristic marker of a human response to divine action — used for the shepherds at Jesus' birth (2:20), for those who witnessed healings (5:25-26; 7:16; 13:13; 17:15; 18:43). Its use by a Roman centurion at the cross places Jesus' death within the same pattern: God has acted, and a witness responds. The most unexpected voice in the chapter speaks the most theologically laden phrase.\f*
\f + \fr 23:47 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq This man was righteous \ft The Roman officer who supervised the execution looks at the cross and says \it „truly this man was righteous"\it*. Modern readers ask whether such a sentence could really come from such a mouth — and what to do with it if it could.

On the believing reading the centurion's word is what conversion under the cross sounds like in real time. A man whose job was death encounters a death that judges him, and tells the truth about it. Christian tradition has heard in this line the first Gentile confession of the Passion — the moment the gospel begins to leak past the boundary of Israel into the empire that has just executed its Lord. Luke writes the scene with theological precision: where Mark gives the centurion \it „Son of God"\it*, Luke writes \fq δίκαιος\fq* — \it righteous\it*, \it innocent\it*, the same word he used of Joseph of Arimathea three verses later and of the centurion Cornelius in Acts 10. The centurion is the fifth and last innocence-verdict of Luke's passion (after Pilate three times and Herod), and the verdict is spoken by the man who drove the nails. From Origen through the medieval mystery plays to twentieth-century writers like Endo and Bonhoeffer, the church has heard in this voice the pattern of every conversion: not the religious recognizing the religious, but the wrong mouth at the wrong time speaking the right word — because the cross has its own way of opening eyes that no preaching could open.

For the reader who can't engage the centurion's word as a historical conversion, the declaration still functions as Luke's verdict on human justice. Pilate could not save him; Herod would not; and at the cross a soldier — the one who has just driven the nails — says what the courts refused. As literary architecture, the scene is Luke's quiet judgment: the truth about this death gets spoken by the wrong mouth, in the wrong uniform, at the wrong time.

And for the reader who can't take even that, critical scholarship reads the line in different keys. Some (Joel Green, François Bovon) hear Luke's editorial signal to his Roman audience that the courts have failed and a centurion has seen what they could not; others see a literary topos — the converted enemy on the page, common in Greco-Roman martyrdom narratives. Whether a historical centurion in fact spoke these words cannot be reached behind the text; what can be said is that Luke's choice of \it δίκαιος\it* is deliberate and load-bearing.

The text gives a Roman the last word at the cross. How far you are willing to hear it as your own is what the scene leaves with you.\f*
\p
\v 48 And the crowds — \add the ones who had come for the spectacle\add* — when they saw what had taken place, they went home beating their chests.
\f + \fr 23:48 \fk TEXT \fq for the spectacle... beating their chests \ft The Greek \fq θεωρίαν\fq* means "spectacle, entertainment" — these people came for a show. The verb \fq τύπτοντες τὰ στήθη\fq* ("beating their breasts") is the gesture of grief and repentance (cf. 18:13: the tax collector "beating his breast"). The transformation is extraordinary: entertainment-seekers become mourners. No one preaches to them. No one explains. The cross itself is the sermon. Luke describes conversion without using the word.\f*
\f + \fr 23:48 \fk LEBEN \fq they went home beating their chests \ft These people came to watch someone die — and something changed them. No sermon. No altar call. No explanation. They simply saw what happened and could not remain the same. Luke describes a transformation that happens before language: people who arrived as spectators left as mourners. The verse names something specific about how moral change actually happens — not usually through argument, but through proximity to what we did not want to see. — A question that lands without theological warmup: what suffering have you been watching from a comfortable distance? The footage you scroll past because looking longer would cost something. The colleague you know is barely holding it together, whom you greet by the coffee machine without ever asking the real question. The relative whose situation everyone has decided not to discuss. The crowd at Golgotha was not malicious; they were curious. They had come to see. Most of us watch suffering the same way — present enough to register, distant enough not to be moved. — A low-threshold practice this week: identify one situation you have been watching from a distance and let it move one step closer. Not solve it. Not fix it. Just stop scrolling past it. Read the article you have been avoiding. Ask the colleague the question you have been not asking. Send the message to the relative whose silence everyone agreed to keep. — For readers inside the tradition, the lineage is long. Simone Weil wrote that the capacity to give attention to suffering — to look at it without turning away — is itself a form of prayer, perhaps the deepest. Dorothy Day's Catholic Worker movement was built on the conviction that proximity is the engine of conversion. The Jesuit tradition of contemplation begins with composition of place — putting yourself inside the scene until it can no longer be observed from outside. — The crowd went home different not because of what they were told, but because of what they could not unsee. — This week: one piece of suffering, let it stop being scenery.\f*
\p
\v 49 All who knew Jesus stood at a distance, watching everything. Among them: the women who had followed him from Galilee.
\f + \fr 23:49 \fk KONTEXT \fq the women who had followed him from Galilee \ft The phrase "at a distance" (\fq μακρόθεν\fq*) echoes Psalm 38:11: "My friends stand aloof from my plague; my neighbors stand far away." These women — named in 8:2-3 (Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Susanna, and others) — have followed Jesus through the entire ministry. They will observe the burial (23:55), prepare spices (23:56), and discover the empty tomb (24:1-3). They are Luke's unbroken witness chain connecting cross, tomb, and resurrection.\f*

\s1 The Burial

\p
\v 50 Then — \add a surprise.\add* A man named Joseph, from Arimathea, came forward.
\v 51 He was a member of the council — but he hadn't consented to any of it. A good man. A righteous man. He had been waiting for the kingdom of God.
\f + \fr 23:50-51 \fk TEXT \fq a member of the council — but he hadn't consented \ft Luke uses the surprise particle \fq ἰδοὺ\fq* to introduce Joseph — an insider who broke ranks. His description as \fq ἀγαθὸς καὶ δίκαιος\fq* ("good and righteous") uses the same word (\fq δίκαιος\fq*) just applied to Jesus in v.47, verbally linking the two men. His legal dissent — "he had not agreed to their decision and action" — is important: not all Sanhedrin members voted for condemnation. This detail counters any collective-guilt reading of the trial.\f*
\f + \fr 23:50b \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq Joseph of Arimathea \ft A council member appears from nowhere, asks Pilate for the body, and lays it in his own unused tomb. Modern readers stand before a contested figure and a quiet act.

On the believing reading Joseph is what the narrative needs and could not have produced for itself: a place. A garden tomb that someone with status gave away when he could have stayed silent. The dignity of burial is the first quiet anticipation of resurrection — a body kept intact because a person dared to dissent. Luke builds the figure as a second \it righteous\it* one (V. 50: \it δίκαιος\it*) beside the centurion of V. 47: a Gentile under the cross, a Jewish councilman at the tomb, both described with the same word — Luke frames the death of Jesus with two righteous outsiders who recognize the Righteous One. Christians from the patristic church onward have heard the body laid in a garden as the deliberate echo of the first man laid in another garden — the resurrection morning will undo, in the same landscape, what the first morning lost. Joseph is also Luke's quiet witness against any reading that makes \it the council\it* a monolith: he sat in the room and did not consent (V. 51), and that small refusal becomes, in the believing reading, a model of faithfulness when everyone else is silent — a pattern the church has recognized in every generation since.

For the reader who can't engage Joseph as historical figure, the scene still works as Luke's literary signature. Joseph mirrors the centurion of v. 47 — both \it δίκαιος\it* — and the death of Jesus is bracketed by two outsiders who say what the institutions won't. As portrait, Joseph is also the witness against any reading of \it the council\it* as a uniform block: one man sat in the room and refused.

And for the reader who can't take even that, historical-critical scholarship is genuinely divided. Arguments for Joseph's historicity (Raymond Brown, N. T. Wright) note that a member of the very body that condemned Jesus would be an unlikely Christian invention, that the place-name \it Arimathea\it* has no obvious symbolic value, and that the 1968 \it Yehohanan\it* heelbone from Giv'at ha-Mivtar — a crucified man given proper burial, with the nail still in the bone — has corrected an older skepticism that the Romans never released crucified bodies. Arguments against (J. D. Crossan, Bart Ehrman) point to typical Roman practice with political prisoners. The sources permit both readings.

The text gives a name and a tomb. How far you are willing to see in that name a man who dissented when it cost something is the choice Luke leaves open in the space he makes for it.\f*
\p
\v 52 He went to Pilate and asked for the body.
\v 53 He took it down, wrapped it in linen cloth, and laid it in a rock-cut tomb where no one had ever been buried.
\f + \fr 23:53 \fk KULTUR \fq a rock-cut tomb where no one had ever been buried \ft Rock-cut tombs were elite burial practice — hundreds have been identified from first-century Jerusalem. Joseph provides his own new tomb for a condemned criminal, an act of both piety and courage. Roman practice typically left crucified bodies on the cross as a deterrent or disposed of them in mass graves. Requesting the body of a crucified man from the Roman governor required both political influence and personal nerve.\f*
\p
\v 54 It was Friday. The Sabbath was about to begin.
\f + \fr 23:54 \fk KULTUR \fq Friday. The Sabbath was about to begin \ft The Greek \fq παρασκευή\fq* ("Preparation Day") is the technical term for Friday — the day before the Sabbath, when all preparation for the day of rest had to be completed before sunset. The Sabbath began at sundown on Friday evening. The urgency of the burial — before sunset — is not about haste but about Torah observance.\f*
\p
\v 55 The women who had followed Jesus from Galilee followed now too. They saw the tomb, saw how his body had been laid — \add noting everything.\add*
\f + \fr 23:55 \fk TEXT \fq noting everything \ft The Greek verb \fq ἐθεάσαντο\fq* is intensive — deliberate, careful observation, not a passing glance. Luke emphasizes the women's role as competent witnesses: they know exactly where the tomb is and exactly how the body was placed. This precise observation establishes the chain of testimony that makes the empty tomb discovery in 24:1-3 credible. When they return on Sunday morning, they will know what they are looking at — and what has changed.\f*
\p
\v 56 Then they went home and prepared spices and ointments for the body.
\p And on the Sabbath, they rested, as the law required.
\f + \fr 23:56 \fk KONTEXT \fq they rested, as the law required \ft Luke's final word before the resurrection: these women are devout, Torah-observant Jews. Even in grief, even with an incomplete burial to finish, they honor the Sabbath commandment (Exodus 20:8-11). The earliest followers of Jesus did not break with Judaism — they observed the law. The spices are ready. The Sabbath holds. Something is about to happen — but not yet. The coiled tension of waiting is entirely contained in the phrase "as the law required."\f*
